# modules.cpp Linux storage emulation

# Status and authority

status: official gives the implemented portions of this document the same
authority as the other specifications. This document specifies the
storage-linux board and the modules under
platforms/linux/storage: a virtual SD card in SPI mode and a virtual
W25Q-family SPI NOR flash on one SPI bus, each keeping its contents in an
image file. They exist, mm.sdcard and mm.spiflash are tested against them,
and apps/socket-smoke runs on them. FAT on the card and littlefs on the flash
need FatFs and littlefs compiled for Linux, which is stage 8 of
drafts/plan-mm-fs-5.mdy; until then mm.fs.fat and mm.fs.littlefs are unbound
on these boards, and a program reaching them is unavailable there.

docs/modules-sdcard.mdy and docs/modules-spiflash.mdy own the drivers;
docs/modules-platform-linux.mdy owns the device map keys.

# Layout

Opening fence
platforms/linux/storage/store      platform.linux.storage.store     BlockStore, MemoryStore, FileStore
platforms/linux/storage/sdcard     platform.linux.storage.sdcard    the virtual SD card
platforms/linux/storage/spiflash   platform.linux.storage.spiflash  the virtual NOR flash
platforms/linux/storage/bus        platform.linux.storage.bus       mm.mcu over both, registering nothing
platforms/linux/storage/device     platform.linux.storage.device    the bus over image files, as mm.mcu
platforms/linux/storage/socket     platform.linux.storage.socket    the card as mm.sdcard.socket
platforms/linux/storage/board      storage-linux-x86_64
platforms/linux/storage/board-aarch64  storage-linux-aarch64
Closing fence

storage-linux is one board on every Linux machine: --board storage-linux,
and storage for short, select the manifest for the machine's architecture,
storage-linux-x86_64 or storage-linux-aarch64, as sdl, epaper, and lcd do.
There are two manifests because a board fixes its SDK and processor.

The store, the chips, and the bus use only the standard library and mm.mcu,
so tests build them over memory. The boards derive from generic-linux-* and
bind mm.mcu to the device, mm.sdcard.socket to the socket, and
platform.linux.map to platform.linux.defaults, the SDK's own map, which reads
MM_LINUX_DEVICE_MAP. Selecting the board is the only way anything pays for
the emulation; the host's own GPIO, SPI, I2C, UART, ADC, and PWM are not
reachable on it.

# Wiring

Opening fence
SPI 0         clock GPIO 18, transmit GPIO 19, receive GPIO 20
SD card       chip select GPIO 23
SPI flash     chip select GPIO 17
Closing fence

The card's wiring is rp2040_geek's TF socket. Both selects start high. A
chip whose select is high ignores the bus and leaves the receive line high; a
byte clocked with both low is answered 0xFF and counted as a wiring error. A
configuration naming other pins or another instance is BadArgument. Time is
the host's steady clock, and delays sleep. mm.mcu's board names the five
GPIOs and the SPI wiring, with the card's select as its chip select.

# The images

sdcard.path, sdcard.size (default 64 MiB), sdcard.kind (sdhc or sdsc),
spiflash.path, and spiflash.size (default 16 MiB) name and size them; without
a path they are sdcard.img and spiflash.img in the working directory. They
are opened at the first mm.mcu call. A missing image is made at its size --
the card zero-filled, the flash all 0xFF, as a new card and an erased chip
read -- and an existing one keeps its contents and its own size. Every block
or page is written through and flushed as it is written, so a program killed
mid-run leaves an image as consistent as the file system on it made it. An
image the chip cannot be -- an SDHC size that is not whole 512 KiB, an SDSC
size that is not whole 256 KiB or is over 1 GiB, a flash size that is not a
power of two from 64 KiB to 16 MiB -- or that cannot be opened leaves that
chip absent, with one line on standard error; an SD size that does not fit is
refused before the file is made.

The images are ordinary files in standard formats. An SD image is a whole
card, which dd writes to a real card and reads back from one, and which host
tools make and inspect:

Opening fence
mkfs.fat -F 32 -C sdcard.img 65536           # 64 MiB, size in KiB
mcopy -i sdcard.img config.txt ::/
mdir -i sdcard.img ::/
fsck.fat -n sdcard.img
Closing fence

A flash image is a W25Q's whole contents, which a programmer writes to a real
chip; littlefs-python makes and reads littlefs images with the 4096-byte
block size mm.fs.littlefs uses:

Opening fence
littlefs-python create data/ spiflash.img --fs-size=16mb --block-size=4096
littlefs-python extract spiflash.img out/ --block-size=4096
Closing fence

mkfs.fat and fsck.fat are dosfstools, mcopy and mdir mtools, and
littlefs-python is on PyPI.

# The virtual SD card

platform.linux.storage.sdcard answers every byte the host clocks with the
byte a card would put on MISO, 0xFF when it has nothing to say, following the
SD Physical Layer Simplified Specification's SPI mode:

- CMD0 with a correct CRC enters SPI mode, idle; a CMD0 with a bad CRC is
  ignored. CMD8 is always CRC-checked, and every command once CMD59 turns
  checking on: a bad CRC7 is the CRC error bit.
- In the idle state only CMD0, CMD1, CMD8, CMD55, ACMD41, CMD58, and CMD59
  are legal; anything else is the illegal-command bit.
- CMD8 echoes its voltage and pattern on a version 2 card and is illegal on a
  version 1 one. ACMD41 answers idle for two polls and ready on the third;
  an SDHC card asked without HCS stays idle.
- CMD58 reports CCS on SDHC. CMD9 sends a CSD -- version 2 on SDHC, version 1
  with READ_BL_LEN 9 and C_SIZE_MULT 7 otherwise -- describing the image,
  and CMD10 a CID naming the emulator, each a data block with its CRC16 and
  its own CRC7.
- CMD16 must be 512. CMD17 reads a block; CMD18 streams blocks until CMD12,
  which is answered after a stuff byte. CMD24 writes one block after a 0xFE
  token; CMD25 writes blocks after 0xFC tokens until the 0xFD stop token.
  Each block is answered 0x05 accepted, 0x0B on a bad CRC16 with checking on,
  or 0x0D on a write error, and then busy. CMD13 answers R2, its second byte
  0x04 while write-protected.
- SDHC addresses blocks and SDSC bytes; an address past the end, or a byte
  address that is not a whole block, is the address-error bit.

Test hooks, not map keys, inject faults: remove (no answer at all), insert
(powered up afresh, contents kept), write_protect, fail_writes_after a number
of blocks, and corrupt_next_read's CRC16. commands() records every command
index received.

# The virtual SPI flash

platform.linux.storage.spiflash is a W25Q at its image's size, answering
JEDEC EF 40 n for 2^n bytes -- EF 40 18 for 16 MiB, a W25Q128. It answers
JEDEC ID, status 1 and 2 (0x05, 0x35), write enable and disable (0x06,
0x04), read and fast read (0x03, 0x0B), page program (0x02), 4, 32, and
64 KiB erases (0x20, 0x52, 0xD8), chip erase (0xC7, 0x60), power-down and
release (0xB9, 0xAB), and reset enable then reset (0x66, 0x99).

- Program ANDs the page's bytes into the array; bits it would set from 0 to
  1 stay and are counted (unerased_bits). Bytes past the page's end wrap to
  its start, and later bytes replace earlier ones in the latch.
- Program and erase take effect when chip select rises, need WEL, clear it,
  and are ignored and counted without it (unenabled_writes).
- After a program or erase, BUSY shows in busy_reads status reads, at least
  one, and any command but a status read meanwhile is ignored and counted
  (busy_violations), so a driver that never polls is caught.
- In power-down only release power-down is answered.

# apps/socket-smoke

Opening fence
sh scripts/build-socket.sh --run                     # storage-linux
MM_LINUX_DEVICE_MAP=/path/to/storage.map sh scripts/build-socket.sh --run
Closing fence

prints the card's geometry and writes, reads back, and restores its last
block; on the default image, socket-smoke: 131072 blocks of 512 bytes.

# Tests

tests/mm/linux checks the chips on their own -- the card's SPI-mode replies
byte by byte, the flash's NOR behaviour, wrap, latches, BUSY, power-down,
and reset -- and with mm.sdcard and mm.spiflash driving them through the bus,
both drivers in turn on one bus with no wiring errors, FileStore's making and
keeping of an image, and the five device-map keys with their refusals.
